307	<section id="s1" class="part">
308	<h2>Not yet read</h2>
309	<p>One hundred and thirty-five items, none of them read. The page holds only what the curator has not read. At 07:45 UTC on 8 November, the curator&rsquo;s comment on change 38 marked changes 37 and 38 read, so they left, and the page starts at change 39. No comment marks a later item read.</p>
310	<p>Climb batch 27 landed on <code>main</code>, and its landing made some sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 16:18 and 16:28 UTC on 7 November. Changes 39 to 41 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: the update rewrote three of its sections for the landing, its judge said to install all three, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 42 to 47 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with what the batch changed in walk, the library and the code generator. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
311	<p>Change 48 came after the landing, at the curator&rsquo;s word in chat. The skill writer made it in two commits to <code>committing.md</code>, at 16:42 and 16:48 UTC on 7 November, the second with the fixes after its cold read.</p>
312	<p>On 8 November, the curator&rsquo;s comments on changes 37 and 39 led to two more commits. At the curator&rsquo;s word of 01:07 UTC, the skill writer made the fixes in one commit at 01:09 UTC. A cold read then found passages that would send a worker wrong, and the skill writer fixed them in a second commit at 02:06 UTC. The two commits changed three passages. One is the passage of change 39, which now shows these changes too, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, 8 November&rdquo;. Another, in <code>SKILL.md</code>, is change 49. The third is the passage of change 37, which the curator has since read.</p>
313	<p>Climb batch 28 landed on <code>main</code> on 8 November, and its landing made more sentences of the skill false or incomplete. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 11:23, 11:27 and 11:36 UTC. Changes 50 and 51 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install two of its sections, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 52 to 57 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>records.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with the landing, and with two findings of the batch&rsquo;s review on how the workers worked: a shallow clone, and a timed run that measured one commit twice. The landing also changed the passage of change 43, which now shows it, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, climb batch 28&rdquo;. Changes 58 to 67 come from cuts. <code>compiler.md</code> had grown to 30,491 characters, past 30,000, the size past which a worker&rsquo;s <code>cat</code> of the part is saved to a file and read again, as two agents of the batch had to do. To bring it under, the skill writer cut text that states no rule, and it is now 29,999 characters. Change 58 defines a term whose pointer a cut took away, and changes 59 to 67 are the cuts. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed all of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
314	<p>Five items of climb batch 27 were new rules when they landed: change 39, a type without its own <code>=</code> compares by strict equivalence; change 40, the static argument written where walk's printed type misleads; change 41, <code>Number</code> declares nothing; change 42, walk's float numerals under strict equivalence; change 48, a commit already made whose footer names a model is left as it is. The other five from that batch state facts that it changed, and change no rule. Change 49 is not a new rule either: it defines two terms. Nor are the changes of 8 November to change 39: it now spells out strict equivalence and three cases where a worker would guess wrong.</p>
315	<p>On the afternoon of 8 November, the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them: they wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language, and told workers to assert it as correct. The curator&rsquo;s rule: <code>revival-changes.md</code> describes the language of revived Fortress and the revival&rsquo;s changes against the team&rsquo;s design, and a behaviour of one path that the language does not give is a defect, with a ledger row, noted in that path&rsquo;s part while it lasts. The curator&rsquo;s decisions of the day, P-171 on string literals and P-172 on numerals with a radix point, give the language&rsquo;s answers. The skill writer then changed the skill in two commits, at 15:32 and 15:37 UTC, the second with the fixes after a cold read. Their changes to the passages of changes 39 to 42, 58 and 67 are folded into those items, each with a third or second reason, &ldquo;Why, 8 November, afternoon&rdquo;. Change 40&rsquo;s and change 42&rsquo;s titles now say what their passages say. Change 68 is the one new item. None of these is a new rule: they correct the rules&rsquo; text, move the behaviour of one path into that path&rsquo;s part, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
316	<p>Of climb batch 28&rsquo;s items, two are new rules: change 51, how to convert an integer into another integer type; change 55, what to do in a shallow clone. One changes a rule: change 56, where the count stage&rsquo;s run after the second pass is now skipped when that pass changed no code. The other fifteen, and the change to change 43, state facts that the batch changed, define a term, fix a word or a citation, or cut text that states no rule.</p>
317	<p>Climb batch 29 landed on <code>main</code> on 9 November. It built the curator&rsquo;s decisions on numerals: a numeral converts where it stands, into the type that its place fixes or else into its default, and an expression of numerals alone folds exactly before the checker, on both paths. It also made two string literals of one text one object on both paths, and fixed the departures of walk that changes 39 to 42 named. So the landing made many sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 12:20, 12:24 and 12:34 UTC. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install three of its sections, with two corrections. Its changes to the passages of changes 39, 40 and 41 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 29&rdquo;, and changes 69 to 73 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>library.md</code> in line with the landing. Its new lists of each path&rsquo;s departures from the rules on numbers stand in the passages of changes 42 and 68, so they are folded into those two items, and changes 74 to 83 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them in the third commit. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 40, 42 and 68 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
318	<p>Of climb batch 29&rsquo;s changes, two are new rules: the change to change 40, under which a numeral converts where it stands, in place of the rule that a numeral has a type of its own; and change 70, under which an expression of numerals alone folds exactly, on both paths. Change 81 puts the last sentence of change 70, that a test of run-time arithmetic needs a variable operand, into the part on tests, in place of a workaround for a defect that the batch fixed. The other thirteen new items, and the changes to changes 39, 41, 42 and 68, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, or fix a word, an example or a citation.</p>
319	<p>Climb batch 29b landed on <code>main</code> at 23:37 UTC on 9 November, as the repair batch after climb batch 29. It built the curator&rsquo;s decisions on a numeral&rsquo;s type: a numeral&rsquo;s type exists only at compile time, with the numeral&rsquo;s four numbers as its static arguments, and a number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over those numbers, which gives one value for each place where the numeral is written. It took away the numeral types&rsquo; getters, and the compiler&rsquo;s prelude now follows the same design. So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 03:30, 03:32 and 03:42 UTC on 10 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install two of its sections, with two corrections. Its changes to the passages of changes 40, 41 and 70 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 29b&rdquo;, and changes 84 and 85 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code> and <code>library.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68, 75 and 78 are folded into those items, and change 86 is its other change. A cold read of both commits then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them in the third commit, which also took the second commit&rsquo;s sentence in <code>library.md</code> back out. Each item shows the net change of the three commits.</p>
320	<p>Of climb batch 29b&rsquo;s changes, five change or add rules: the change to change 40, under which a numeral&rsquo;s type has its four numbers as static arguments and a program&rsquo;s own number type takes a numeral through a coercion generic over them, in place of a coercion that reads getters; the change to change 41, which gives the rule&rsquo;s answer for <code>=</code> between a number and a numeral, and the form to write; the change to change 70, under which a folded constant reaches a program&rsquo;s own type only through a coercion from its default type; the change to change 75, under which a numeral type&rsquo;s numbers, used as values, are <code>ZZ</code> values; and change 86, under which the prelude&rsquo;s numerals are the one exception to the rule that adds nothing to the prelude. The others, changes 84 and 85 and the changes to changes 42, 68 and 78, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
321	<p>Climb batch 30 landed on <code>main</code> on 13 November. It made the compiled path run a call&rsquo;s arguments as parallel tasks, as the language&rsquo;s rule asks, fixed defects of the compiled <code>atomic</code> block and <code>try</code>, and fixed four departures on numerals that changes 40, 42 and 68 named (rows 958, 967, 971 and 974). So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, two at 22:41 and one at 22:46 UTC on 13 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install six of its seven sections, and held one, &ldquo;Static parameters, <code>where</code> clauses and sizes&rdquo;, which keeps its text. Beside the landing&rsquo;s one correction, on a numeral that rounds to an infinity, this update took in the findings of its own cold read, so it reworded sentences in each of the six sections. Its changes to the passages of changes 40, 41, 51, 70 and 85 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 30&rdquo;, and changes 87 to 100 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>gate.md</code> and <code>tests-writing.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68 and 78 are folded into those items, and changes 101 to 105 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found nineteen passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed sixteen in the third commit and left three, which the items of changes 89, 96 and 98 name. Each item shows the net change of the three commits.</p>
322	<p>Of climb batch 30&rsquo;s changes, three are new rules: change 102, under which no compiled program relies on the order in which a call&rsquo;s arguments are evaluated; change 103, under which an <code>atomic</code> block or a <code>try</code> is bound in a statement of its own before it is passed as a later argument; and change 105, under which a test of parallelism under walk needs a range longer than 16. The change to change 40 adds the way to get an infinity. The other sixteen new items, and the changes to changes 41, 42, 51, 68, 70, 78 and 85, state facts that the batch changed, reword sentences that a reader could take two ways, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
323	<p>Climb batch 32 landed on <code>main</code> on 16 November. On both paths, it let a numeral run a more specific declaration than the one that fits its default type. It made the checker take the unwritten static argument of a reduction such as <code>SUM</code> from the reduction&rsquo;s expected type, or else from its body. It let a reference to a generic function take its instance from the arrow type that receives it. And it fixed walk&rsquo;s answer for <code>u = 0</code>, which changes 41 and 42 named (rows 973 and 966). So the landing made sentences of the skill false again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 07:10, 07:13 and 07:19 UTC on 16 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install the three sections that it rewrote, &ldquo;Static parameters, <code>where</code> clauses and sizes&rdquo;, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo; and &ldquo;Reductions and comprehensions&rdquo;, and held none. Its changes to the passages of changes 41, 71 and 99 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 32&rdquo;, and changes 106 to 108 are its other changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>gate.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>library.md</code> and <code>tests-writing.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 42, 68 and 76 are folded into those items, and changes 109 to 117 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found twenty-three passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed twenty-two in the third commit and left one, which the item of change 71 names. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 76 and 99 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
324	<p>Of climb batch 32&rsquo;s changes, three change or add rules of the language as built: change 108, under which a numeral can run a more specific declaration than the one that fits its default type; the change to change 71, under which a tie between declarations that apply only by coercion goes to the one that takes the call&rsquo;s numerals at their default; and the change to change 99, under which the checker takes a reduction&rsquo;s unwritten static argument from the expected type or the body, and a worker writes the argument at four places only. The change to change 76 states the rule of change 108 in <code>SKILL.md</code>. Change 116 changes an instruction: the element type of <code>SUM</code> and <code>PROD</code> is written in the library, and no longer everywhere. Change 117 is a new rule for the Fortress of a test: parenthesise a call or a subscript before a superscript. The other nine new items, and the changes to changes 41, 42 and 68, state facts that the batch changed, name departures and traps that it found, each with the way round it, or reword a sentence that a reader could take two ways.</p>
325	<p>On the evening of 16 November, the skill writer changed the skill in two commits, at 23:15 and 23:20 UTC, the second with the fixes after a cold read. They put into the skill corrections that the curator made between 9 October and 16 November, to what the workers did or to the skill&rsquo;s text. Changes 118 to 123 are the new items, one for each rule. The change to the rule on the compiler&rsquo;s prelude touches the passage of change 86, so it is folded into that item, with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, 16 November&rdquo;, and change 86 has a new title. The two commits also cut or shortened sentences that state no rule, to keep the files&rsquo; sizes. Where such a sentence stands in the section of a new item, that item shows it. Two stand in the passages of changes 100 and 105, and are folded into those items. Change 124 lists the rest. Each item shows the net change of the two commits.</p>
326	<p>Of these changes, two are new rules: change 119, under which each test case is asserted in one test only; and change 120, under which an assertion&rsquo;s message is one short line. Three change a rule&rsquo;s meaning: change 118, under which a probe&rsquo;s patch, report and programs are committed, where the skill counted probe programs as scratch; change 122, under which the library takes a general declaration that a program had to write for itself, and a declaration of the program&rsquo;s own domain stays in the program; and the change to change 86, under which the compiler&rsquo;s prelude has a second exception. Change 121 rewords a sentence on an <code>XXX</code> test that read as an exception, and change 123 corrects a fact under the advice to write every parameter type. Change 124, and the changes to changes 100 and 105, cut words that state no rule.</p>
327	<p>Climb batch 31 landed its first run on <code>main</code> at 04:26 UTC on 17 November. It taught the code generator <code>label</code>, <code>exit</code> and the atomic expression <code>atomic e</code>. It kept a compiled object&rsquo;s <code>var</code> field in a cell that the object holds, so that an <code>atomic</code> block tracks it. It made the compiled code cast <code>self</code> where the checker reads it as more than its trait, so that a compiled body of a self-typed trait that returns <code>self</code> runs. It made the code generator write each inherited method once. And it made the checker and walk&rsquo;s load check judge more pairs of declarations by the Return Type Rule. So the landing made sentences of the skill false or incomplete again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 07:57, 08:00 and 08:11 UTC on 17 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install the whole text, with one correction, and held no section. Changes 125 and 126 are its changes. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>gate.md</code> and <code>interpreter.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 53, 54, 68, 101, 103, 104 and 113 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 31&rdquo;, and changes 127 and 130 to 138 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found thirty passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed twenty-six in the third commit and left four, which the items of changes 125, 131 and 135 and the change to change 113 name. The third commit also moved two sentences of the second into <code>SKILL.md</code>, which every worker reads: change 128, and the change to change 49. And it named the Return Type Rule where <code>SKILL.md</code> states it, change 129, and put a limit of the checker beside the rule to declare a method again, the change to change 94. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 49, 53, 54, 94, 101, 103 and 104 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
328	<p>None of climb batch 31&rsquo;s changes is a new rule of the language. The change to change 49 states a rule of the specification that the skill did not have: give a value object no <code>var</code> field, since nothing refuses one. Change 125 narrows a sentence that stated the rule of the curator&rsquo;s decision P-152 too widely, to what that decision says. The other new items, and the changes to changes 53, 54, 68, 94, 101, 103, 104 and 113, state facts that the batch changed, name traps and departures that it found, with the way round each where one is known, name a rule, or take out a quoted file path that every agent received with the skill.</p>
329	<p>Climb batch 31b, the second run of climb batch 31, landed on <code>main</code> between 09:14 and 09:25 UTC on 17 November. It made a compiled method whose one parameter is a tuple run. It made five of the number types, <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>ZZ</code>, <code>NN64</code> and <code>RR64</code>, each one value object in the interpreter&rsquo;s library and in walk, as the curator&rsquo;s decision P-178 asks, so that the team&rsquo;s native objects <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>Float</code>, <code>BigNum</code> and <code>UnsignedLong</code> are gone. And it made a numeral that ends a block after a local declaration, or that stands in a tuple, convert where it stands at a declared type on the compiled path. So the landing made sentences of the skill false or incomplete again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, two at 14:25 and one at 14:33 UTC on 17 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install the whole text, with three corrections, and held no section. Its changes to the passages of changes 39, 40, 41, 50, 84, 85, 87, 94, 95 and 126 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 31b&rdquo;, and change 139 is its other change. The second commit brought <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code> and <code>library.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 62, 68 and 138 are folded into those items, and changes 140 to 146 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found nineteen passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed fourteen in the third commit, left four, which the items of changes 84, 95 and 126 and the change to change 138 name, and left one as a decision not taken, which the change to change 138 names too. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 50, 62, 84, 85, 87 and 138 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
330	<p>Of climb batch 31b&rsquo;s changes, two state a rule of the curator&rsquo;s decision P-178 that the skill did not have: the change to change 85, under which a program extends no number type and declares its own number type below <code>Number</code>; and change 139, under which a program writes <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>RR64</code>, <code>ZZ</code> or <code>NN64</code> where a team test or an old note names <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>Float</code>, <code>BigNum</code> or <code>UnsignedLong</code>. The change to change 87 states two instructions that <code>tests-writing.md</code> already gave: where the rules decide a case that a ledger row holds, an <code>XXX</code> test asserts their answer, and where they do not, a plain test asserts today&rsquo;s behaviour beside the row. The change to change 126 makes clear that its instruction on <code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code> holds today too, not only from the switch-over. The other new items, and the changes to changes 39 to 41, 50, 62, 68, 84, 94, 95 and 138, state facts that the batch changed, name traps and departures that it found, with the way round each where one is known, or reword sentences that a reader could take two ways.</p>
331	<p>Climb batch 33 landed its first run on <code>main</code> between 21:28 and 22:36 UTC on 17 November. It taught <code>fortress compile</code> to read the one library in place of the compiler&rsquo;s prelude, where the environment variable <code>FORTRESS_LIBRARY</code> is set to <code>one</code>, with a caches folder of its own, a test folder and a fifth track of <code>ant testFast</code>. It made a <code>typecase</code> arm at a name that names no type a static error on both paths. It made the compiled code cast <code>self</code> at a conditional&rsquo;s arm and at a closure&rsquo;s result, and the class loader choose a call&rsquo;s opcode without loading a class, so that three shapes of a self-typed trait&rsquo;s compiled body that failed when the class loaded now run. And it repaired defects of the compiled path: a generic member under a <code>comprises</code> clause, a call on a value typed by an intersection, and four smaller gaps of the checker, <code>x[5]^2</code> among them. So the landing made sentences of the skill false or incomplete again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 01:11, 01:14 and 01:23 UTC on 18 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install the whole text, and held no section. Its changes to the passages of changes 96, 126 and 139 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 33&rdquo;, and it made no new item. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>build-and-caches.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code>, <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>library.md</code>, <code>tests-running.md</code> and <code>tests-writing.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 68, 80, 117 and 121 are folded into those items, and changes 147 to 163 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found twenty-five passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed twenty-one in the third commit, one of them by cutting a record number, and left four, which the items of changes 160, 161 and 162 and the change to change 126 name. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 80, 121, 126 and 139 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
332	<p>None of climb batch 33&rsquo;s changes is a new rule of the language. Change 149 gives an instruction for the new variable: set the environment variable, not the Java property alone, and run the library order without it. Change 163 gives one for the new test folder: a plain test there has a <code>compile</code> command only. The change to change 139 gives the form to write since the batch&rsquo;s fix: an arm over every value is <code>x: Any =&gt;</code>. The change to change 126 drops three workarounds and the advice to declare one&rsquo;s own <code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code>, since the shapes that needed them now run. The other new items, and the changes to changes 68, 80, 96, 117 and 121, state facts that the batch changed, name traps and gaps that it found, each with the way round it where one is known, or cut a limit that the batch took away.</p>
333	<p>Climb batch 33b, the second run of climb batch 33, landed on <code>main</code> between 05:32 and 06:41 UTC on 18 November. It declared the seven number types of the compiler&rsquo;s prelude as value objects, as the interpreter&rsquo;s library already does, so that both paths now refuse a program&rsquo;s object below one of them. It taught the code generator a varargs parameter, where the one library is in use. And it made a compiled call at an assumption of an imported api&rsquo;s trait find the trait&rsquo;s method by its api, so that the call runs the trait&rsquo;s body. So the landing made sentences of the skill false or incomplete again. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 08:53, 08:54 and 09:01 UTC on 18 November. The first installed the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install the opening and &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;, and held &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, which keeps its text. Its changes to the passages of changes 41, 85 and 87 are folded into those items, each with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, climb batch 33b&rdquo;, and it made no new item. The second commit brought <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>compiler.md</code> and <code>revival-changes.md</code> in line with the landing. Its changes to the passages of changes 45, 68, 71 and 86 are folded into those items, and changes 164 to 172 are its other changes. A cold read of both commits then found thirteen passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search. The skill writer fixed eleven in the third commit and left two, which the changes to changes 45 and 87 name. Each item shows the net change of the three commits. Changes 45, 71 and 85 have new titles, since their passages now say other things.</p>
334	<p>None of climb batch 33b&rsquo;s changes is a new rule of the language. The change to change 87 gives the same rule as two instructions, where a ban and the sentence after it read as a contradiction. The change to change 86 states as built the second exception of the curator&rsquo;s decision of 16 November: the prelude&rsquo;s seven number types are value objects. Change 167 and the change to change 71 each give the form to write where a trap stands: give a varargs declaration its own name, and declare a function at the parameter&rsquo;s arrow type. The other new items, and the changes to changes 41, 45, 68 and 85, state facts that the batch changed, name traps and limits that it found, each with the way round it where one is known, or bound a sentence to the path where it holds.</p>
335	<p>On the morning of 19 November, the skill writer changed the skill in two commits, at 05:51 and 05:55 UTC, the second with the fixes after a cold read. They put the curator&rsquo;s rule of 18 November on a test&rsquo;s first run into <code>tests-writing.md</code>, &ldquo;The order: the test, its failure, the fix, the pass&rdquo;. Change 173 is the new item: step 2 of that section. The change to the paragraph on the report, after the steps, touches the passage of change 118, so it is folded into that item, with a reason headed &ldquo;Why, 19 November&rdquo;, and change 118 has a new title. Each item shows the net change of the two commits.</p>
336	<p>Change 173 is a new rule: before a test&rsquo;s first run, a worker states in a message what the harness should report, in a line that starts <code>Expected failure:</code>, and the run must fail that way. A run that passes, or fails another way, is explained and corrected, and the expected failure is stated again, before the fix. The change to change 118 is the same rule&rsquo;s part in the report, which now quotes the expected failure and gives the cause of each run that failed another way.</p>
337	<p>Each item shows the skill&rsquo;s current text, as it stands on <code>main</code>, against the text before the item&rsquo;s first commit. When a later commit changes an item&rsquo;s passage, the change is folded into that item, with its own reason, and does not become a new item. The words that came in are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. The next new change will be change 174.</p>
