302	<section id="about" class="part">
303	<h2>What this page is</h2>
304	<p>The <code>fortress-repo</code> skill holds the rules that bind every worker in the repository. This page shows each change that landed in it, as a diff with its reason, so that the curator can check what the workers are now told at any time. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat.</p>
305	</section>
306	
307	<section id="s1" class="part">
308	<h2>Not yet read</h2>
309	<p>One hundred and thirty-five items, none of them read. The page holds only what the curator has not read. At 07:45 UTC on 8 November, the curator&rsquo;s comment on change 38 marked changes 37 and 38 read, so they left, and the page starts at change 39. No comment marks a later item read.</p>
310	<p>Climb batch 27 landed on <code>main</code>, and its landing made some sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 16:18 and 16:28 UTC on 7 November. Changes 39 to 41 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: the update rewrote three of its sections for the landing, its judge said to install all three, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 42 to 47 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>tests-writing.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with what the batch changed in walk, the library and the code generator. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed most of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
311	<p>Change 48 came after the landing, at the curator&rsquo;s word in chat. The skill writer made it in two commits to <code>committing.md</code>, at 16:42 and 16:48 UTC on 7 November, the second with the fixes after its cold read.</p>
312	<p>On 8 November, the curator&rsquo;s comments on changes 37 and 39 led to two more commits. At the curator&rsquo;s word of 01:07 UTC, the skill writer made the fixes in one commit at 01:09 UTC. A cold read then found passages that would send a worker wrong, and the skill writer fixed them in a second commit at 02:06 UTC. The two commits changed three passages. One is the passage of change 39, which now shows these changes too, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, 8 November&rdquo;. Another, in <code>SKILL.md</code>, is change 49. The third is the passage of change 37, which the curator has since read.</p>
313	<p>Climb batch 28 landed on <code>main</code> on 8 November, and its landing made more sentences of the skill false or incomplete. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, at 11:23, 11:27 and 11:36 UTC. Changes 50 and 51 come from the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: its judge said to install two of its sections, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 52 to 57 bring <code>interpreter.md</code>, <code>SKILL.md</code>, <code>records.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with the landing, and with two findings of the batch&rsquo;s review on how the workers worked: a shallow clone, and a timed run that measured one commit twice. The landing also changed the passage of change 43, which now shows it, with a second reason, &ldquo;Why, climb batch 28&rdquo;. Changes 58 to 67 come from cuts. <code>compiler.md</code> had grown to 30,491 characters, past 30,000, the size past which a worker&rsquo;s <code>cat</code> of the part is saved to a file and read again, as two agents of the batch had to do. To bring it under, the skill writer cut text that states no rule, and it is now 29,999 characters. Change 58 defines a term whose pointer a cut took away, and changes 59 to 67 are the cuts. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed all of them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
314	<p>Five items of climb batch 27 were new rules when they landed: change 39, a type without its own <code>=</code> compares by strict equivalence; change 40, the static argument written where walk's printed type misleads; change 41, <code>Number</code> declares nothing; change 42, walk's float numerals under strict equivalence; change 48, a commit already made whose footer names a model is left as it is. The other five from that batch state facts that it changed, and change no rule. Change 49 is not a new rule either: it defines two terms. Nor are the changes of 8 November to change 39: it now spells out strict equivalence and three cases where a worker would guess wrong.</p>
315	<p>On the afternoon of 8 November, the curator read changes 39 to 44 and refused them: they wrote the behaviour of one implementation as rules of the language, and told workers to assert it as correct. The curator&rsquo;s rule: <code>revival-changes.md</code> describes the language of revived Fortress and the revival&rsquo;s changes against the team&rsquo;s design, and a behaviour of one path that the language does not give is a defect, with a ledger row, noted in that path&rsquo;s part while it lasts. The curator&rsquo;s decisions of the day, P-171 on string literals and P-172 on numerals with a radix point, give the language&rsquo;s answers. The skill writer then changed the skill in two commits, at 15:32 and 15:37 UTC, the second with the fixes after a cold read. Their changes to the passages of changes 39 to 42, 58 and 67 are folded into those items, each with a third or second reason, &ldquo;Why, 8 November, afternoon&rdquo;. Change 40&rsquo;s and change 42&rsquo;s titles now say what their passages say. Change 68 is the one new item. None of these is a new rule: they correct the rules&rsquo; text, move the behaviour of one path into that path&rsquo;s part, or cut words that state no rule.</p>
316	<p>Of climb batch 28&rsquo;s items, two are new rules: change 51, how to convert an integer into another integer type; change 55, what to do in a shallow clone. One changes a rule: change 56, where the count stage&rsquo;s run after the second pass is now skipped when that pass changed no code. The other fifteen, and the change to change 43, state facts that the batch changed, define a term, fix a word or a citation, or cut text that states no rule.</p>
